In this lesson we'll look at families of curves that all share the same gradient function, and you'll see why they always differ by just a single number. So picture a whole bunch of parabolas stacked on top of each other, like layers of a cake. Every single one is the square of x plus some constant, and as you slide that constant up or down, the whole curve just shifts up or down with it. Because they only move vertically, every curve in the family has exactly the same gradient at any given x value, so they're really just vertical shifts of one another. Now here's the cool part. The whole family is described by one free parameter, that constant we keep shifting. So if you want to pick out just one curve from the bunch, you only need one extra clue. It could be a boundary condition, like the curve passes through a particular point, say three, seven. Or it could be an initial condition, like the value of the function is five when x is zero. Either way, you just plug that information into your equation and solve for the constant, and boom, you've nailed down your unique curve. The key takeaway is this. One free parameter needs one equation to fix it. Now let's see it in action.